## Deploy to Render

`render.yaml` is a Blueprint for the web service and a daily clean-up job.
[titan](https://github.com/hugorodgerbrown/django-titan-template/tree/main/titan)
sets everything up: run `titan deploy {{ project_slug }}` once the repo is on GitHub. Both services read their
settings from two env groups, `titan` (shared by every Titan app) and
`{{ project_slug }}` (this app's own). titan makes both before the Blueprint
is deployed.

By hand instead:

1. Create a database and role for the project on your Postgres instance.
2. Create the env group `titan` with `DJANGO_DEBUG=0`, `PYTHON_VERSION=3.14`,
   `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS`, `DJANGO_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` and the email relay
   (`EMAIL_HOST`, `EMAIL_PORT`, `EMAIL_HOST_USER`, `EMAIL_USE_TLS`).
3. Create the env group `{{ project_slug }}` with `DATABASE_URL` (the
   instance's internal URL with this project's role and database),
   `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`, `ACCOUNTS_EMAIL_KEY`, `EMAIL_HOST_PASSWORD` and
   `DEFAULT_FROM_EMAIL`. Without the email settings nobody can sign in.
   `ACCOUNTS_EMAIL_KEY` finds every account by its address: keep a copy, and
   never change it (docs/accounts.md).
4. In Render, New → Blueprint, pick this repository.
5. On a custom domain, set `SITE_URL` (links in emails, and the passkey
   origin) in the app's group.
6. The build runs `collectstatic`; each deploy runs `migrate` first;
   `/livez` is Render's health check (the process is up); `/healthz`
   also reads the database, for an uptime monitor.
